<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Zab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Upper Zab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Zab is a river rising in the mountains of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and flowing through Iraqi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Its valleys formed the heartland of Kurdish principalities such as Bahdinan and Soran. In 750 its banks saw the Battle of the Zab, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> defeated the last Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zab.jpg" alt="Great Zab" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jamesdale10 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greater_Zab_River_near_Erbil_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
